  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-17-4",
    "page_path": "/explore/mining/building-materials/",
    "name": "Building Materials",
    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/mining/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's building-material industries connect mineral resources with cement, ceramics, glass, masonry, stone, and insulation. Understanding the sector requires separating extracted minerals from manufactured products and distinguishing production volumes from the performance of completed buildings.",
    "tags": ["Building materials", "Iranian industry", "Cement", "Ceramics", "Glass", "Gypsum", "Insulation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:08:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:08:35Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Building Materials in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran's building-material industries, from cement and gypsum to ceramics, glass, stone, and insulation, with clear production distinctions."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From mineral resources to construction products",
        "content": "A useful way to explore Iran's building-material industries is to follow what happens to their mineral inputs. Some materials are crushed and graded; others are shaped and fired, melted and formed, or chemically transformed into binders. These routes create different products and different demands for industrial heat, electricity, handling, and quality control. EPA process references provide the technical distinctions used here; they describe manufacturing principles rather than a census of Iranian plants. [s2][s3][s4][s5][s6][s8]\n\nIran provides a substantial setting for those relationships. USGS documents cement manufacturing and gypsum extraction, identifying Semnan in north-central Iran as a leading gypsum-producing province in its account of 2023. This connects the sector to both heavy manufacturing and geographically specific mineral resources. It does not mean that all building products share the same production geography. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6", "s8"]
      },
      {
        "id": "binders",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cement and gypsum: different kinds of transformation",
        "content": "Cement belongs to the binder branch. Its manufacture combines raw-material preparation, high-temperature processing, and final grinding. The intermediate product is clinker, which is subsequently processed into cement. Keeping those stages distinct matters when reading industrial statistics: clinker and finished cement are successive products, so adding their tonnages would double-count material moving through the chain. [s2]\n\nGypsum follows another route. It is calcium sulfate dihydrate, and partial dehydration produces the material commonly called stucco for plaster and wallboard manufacture. Raw gypsum can also enter cement production as an additive. A gypsum mine, a plaster works, and a wallboard factory therefore occupy different positions in a supply chain even when they depend on the same mineral. Mine output alone cannot measure the volume of finished interiors or construction products. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ceramics-and-bricks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Ceramics and bricks: shaping before firing",
        "content": "Clay-based products offer another industrial pathway. Brick manufacture typically prepares and blends clay or shale, forms the material, and then dries, fires, and cools it. Drying and firing are separate operations: removing moisture is not the same transformation as developing the fired product. The EPA description also shows that raw-material composition, kiln conditions, and fuel can affect emissions. These variables make a single environmental claim for every brick factory inappropriate. [s4]\n\nCeramics encompass a broader family of inorganic, nonmetallic materials. Traditional ceramic bodies use clay and other minerals, while more specialized compositions can serve technical purposes. Architectural products should consequently be distinguished from household pottery and technical ceramics rather than combined indiscriminately into one market category. [s5]\n\nAn Iranian example is Mahceram, whose company account describes a progression from glazed tiles toward porcelain surfaces and identifies a Yazd office. This supports a concrete example of product diversification in the Iranian industry. It does not independently substantiate the company's claims of national leadership or prove Yazd's share of total output. [s7]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5", "s7"]
      },
      {
        "id": "glass-stone-insulation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Glass, stone, and insulation require different measures",
        "content": "Soda-lime glass is made from a batch that includes sand, limestone, soda ash, and broken glass, known as cullet. The manufacturing sequence moves through preparation, melting, forming, and finishing. Flat glass and container glass share important upstream operations but differ in how the molten material is shaped. A national total for all glass therefore cannot automatically describe the supply of architectural glazing. [s6]\n\nStone also needs careful classification. EPA describes crushed-stone processing as a sequence involving size reduction, screening, and handling. That is a different product concept from the building-stone branch in this guide, which concerns stone selected and finished for architectural use. An aggregate statistic should not be presented as a measure of finished stone slabs. [s8]\n\nInsulation adds a further distinction. Mineral wool can be manufactured from rock or suitable slags by melting the feed, forming fibers, and producing the final material. Its forms include batts, blankets, and loose fibers. This illustrates why insulation is a family of products with particular functions, rather than simply another name for a quarried mineral. The reference establishes the process, not Iran's current production mix. [s9]",
        "source_ids": ["s6", "s8", "s9"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-sector",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read the industries as connected, distinct branches",
        "content": "The practical conclusion is to compare like with like. Cement, raw gypsum, fired tiles, architectural glass, and insulation describe different stages and functions; their output figures cannot be treated as interchangeable indicators of construction activity. The child pages separate these branches so that each can be examined using an appropriate product definition. [s2][s3][s5][s6][s9]\n\nEnvironmental comparisons need the same discipline. Quarry and handling dust, kiln emissions, and the energy used in melting are different issues. The EPA references explain where such impacts can arise, but their process descriptions and emission factors do not establish emissions at an Iranian facility. Evidence about an individual plant must accompany any claim about its actual performance. [s2][s4][s5][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4", "s5", "s6", "s9"]
      }
    ],
